@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.847 = private unnamed_addr constant [1359 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of multi-character strings in `.strip()`, `.lstrip()`, and\0A`.rstrip()` calls.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AAll characters in the call to `.strip()`, `.lstrip()`, or `.rstrip()` are\0Aremoved from the leading and trailing ends of the string. If the string\0Acontains multiple characters, the reader may be misled into thinking that\0Aa prefix or suffix is being removed, rather than a set of characters.\0A\0AIn Python 3.9 and later, you can use `str.removeprefix` and\0A`str.removesuffix` to remove an exact prefix or suffix from a string,\0Arespectively, which should be preferred when possible.\0A\0A## Known problems\0AAs a heuristic, this rule only flags multi-character strings that contain\0Aduplicate characters. This allows usages like `.strip(\22xyz\22)`, which\0Aremoves all occurrences of the characters `x`, `y`, and `z` from the\0Aleading and trailing ends of the string, but not `.strip(\22foo\22)`.\0A\0AThe use of unique, multi-character strings may be intentional and\0Aconsistent with the intent of `.strip()`, `.lstrip()`, or `.rstrip()`,\0Awhile the use of duplicate-character strings is very likely to be a\0Amistake.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0A\22text.txt\22.strip(\22.txt\22)  # \22e\22\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0A\22text.txt\22.removesuffix(\22.txt\22)  # \22text\22\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `str.strip`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#str.strip)\0A", align 1
