@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.858 = private unnamed_addr constant [18 x i8] c"useless-expression", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.859 = private unnamed_addr constant [1104 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for useless expressions.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AUseless expressions have no effect on the program, and are often included\0Aby mistake. Assign a useless expression to a variable, or remove it\0Aentirely.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0A1 + 1\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afoo = 1 + 1\0A```\0A\0A## Notebook behavior\0AFor Jupyter Notebooks, this rule is not applied to the last top-level expression in a cell.\0AThis is because it's common to have a notebook cell that ends with an expression,\0Awhich will result in the `repr` of the evaluated expression being printed as the cell's output.\0A\0A## Known problems\0AThis rule ignores expression types that are commonly used for their side\0Aeffects, such as function calls.\0A\0AHowever, if a seemingly useless expression (like an attribute access) is\0Aneeded to trigger a side effect, consider assigning it to an anonymous\0Avariable, to indicate that the return value is intentionally ignored.\0A\0AFor example, given:\0A```python\0Awith errors.ExceptionRaisedContext():\0A    obj.attribute\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Awith errors.ExceptionRaisedContext():\0A    _ = obj.attribute\0A```\0A", align 1
