@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1045 = private unnamed_addr constant [945 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for dunder methods that have no special meaning in Python 3.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AMisspelled or no longer supported dunder name methods may cause your code to not function\0Aas expected.\0A\0ASince dunder methods are associated with customizing the behavior\0Aof a class in Python, introducing a dunder method such as `__foo__`\0Athat diverges from standard Python dunder methods could potentially\0Aconfuse someone reading the code.\0A\0AThis rule will detect all methods starting and ending with at least\0Aone underscore (e.g., `_str_`), but ignores known dunder methods (like\0A`__init__`), as well as methods that are marked with `@override`.\0A\0AAdditional dunder methods names can be allowed via the\0A[`lint.pylint.allow-dunder-method-names`] setting.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Aclass Foo:\0A    def __init_(self): ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Aclass Foo:\0A    def __init__(self): ...\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `lint.pylint.allow-dunder-method-names`\0A", align 1
