@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1065 = private unnamed_addr constant [22 x i8] c"modified-iterating-set", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1066 = private unnamed_addr constant [1144 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for loops in which a `set` is modified during iteration.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIf a `set` is modified during iteration, it will cause a `RuntimeError`.\0A\0AIf you need to modify a `set` within a loop, consider iterating over a copy\0Aof the `set` instead.\0A\0A## Known problems\0AThis rule favors false negatives over false positives. Specifically, it\0Awill only detect variables that can be inferred to be a `set` type based on\0Alocal type inference, and will only detect modifications that are made\0Adirectly on the variable itself (e.g., `set.add()`), as opposed to\0Amodifications within other function calls (e.g., `some_function(set)`).\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Anums = {1, 2, 3}\0Afor num in nums:\0A    nums.add(num + 5)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Anums = {1, 2, 3}\0Afor num in nums.copy():\0A    nums.add(num + 5)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis fix is always unsafe because it changes the program\E2\80\99s behavior. Replacing the\0Aoriginal set with a copy during iteration allows code that would previously raise a\0A`RuntimeError` to run without error.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `set`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#set)\0A", align 1
