@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1118 = private unnamed_addr constant [1214 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for classes that inherit from both `str` and `enum.Enum`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0APython 3.11 introduced `enum.StrEnum`, which is preferred over inheriting\0Afrom both `str` and `enum.Enum`.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Aimport enum\0A\0A\0Aclass Foo(str, enum.Enum): ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Aimport enum\0A\0A\0Aclass Foo(enum.StrEnum): ...\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0A\0APython 3.11 introduced a [breaking change] for enums that inherit from both\0A`str` and `enum.Enum`. Consider the following enum:\0A\0A```python\0Afrom enum import Enum\0A\0A\0Aclass Foo(str, Enum):\0A    BAR = \22bar\22\0A```\0A\0AIn Python 3.11, the formatted representation of `Foo.BAR` changed as\0Afollows:\0A\0A```python\0A# Python 3.10\0Af\22{Foo.BAR}\22  # > bar\0A# Python 3.11\0Af\22{Foo.BAR}\22  # > Foo.BAR\0A```\0A\0AMigrating from `str` and `enum.Enum` to `enum.StrEnum` will restore the\0Aprevious behavior, such that:\0A\0A```python\0Afrom enum import StrEnum\0A\0A\0Aclass Foo(StrEnum):\0A    BAR = \22bar\22\0A\0A\0Af\22{Foo.BAR}\22  # > bar\0A```\0A\0AAs such, migrating to `enum.StrEnum` will introduce a behavior change for\0Acode that relies on the Python 3.11 behavior.\0A\0A## References\0A- [enum.StrEnum](https://docs.python.org/3/library/enum.html#enum.StrEnum)\0A\0A[breaking change]: https://blog.pecar.me/python-enum\0A", align 1
