@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1207 = private unnamed_addr constant [25 x i8] c"assert-with-print-message", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1208 = private unnamed_addr constant [1115 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `assert expression, print(message)`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIf an `assert x, y` assertion fails, the Python interpreter raises an\0A`AssertionError`, and the evaluated value of `y` is used as the contents of\0Athat assertion error. The `print` function always returns `None`, however,\0Aso the evaluated value of a call to `print` will always be `None`.\0A\0AUsing a `print` call in this context will therefore output the message to\0A`stdout`, before raising an empty `AssertionError(None)`. Instead, remove\0Athe `print` and pass the message directly as the second expression,\0Aallowing `stderr` to capture the message in a well-formatted context.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aassert False, print(\22This is a message\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aassert False, \22This is a message\22\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as changing the second expression\0Awill result in a different `AssertionError` message being raised, as well as\0Aa change in `stdout` output.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `assert`](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/simple_stmts.html#the-assert-statement)\0A", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1209 = private unnamed_addr constant [60 x i8] c"`print()` call in `assert` statement is likely unintentional", align 1
