@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1225 = private unnamed_addr constant [1413 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for mutable objects passed as a value argument to `dict.fromkeys`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AAll values in the dictionary created by the `dict.fromkeys` method\0Arefer to the same instance of the provided object. If that object is\0Amodified, all values are modified, which can lead to unexpected behavior.\0AFor example, if the empty list (`[]`) is provided as the default value,\0Aall values in the dictionary will use the same list; as such, appending to\0Aany one entry will append to all entries.\0A\0AInstead, use a comprehension to generate a dictionary with distinct\0Ainstances of the default value.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Acities = dict.fromkeys([\22UK\22, \22Poland\22], [])\0Acities[\22UK\22].append(\22London\22)\0Acities[\22Poland\22].append(\22Poznan\22)\0Aprint(cities)  # {'UK': ['London', 'Poznan'], 'Poland': ['London', 'Poznan']}\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Acities = {country: [] for country in [\22UK\22, \22Poland\22]}\0Acities[\22UK\22].append(\22London\22)\0Acities[\22Poland\22].append(\22Poznan\22)\0Aprint(cities)  # {'UK': ['London'], 'Poland': ['Poznan']}\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as the edit will change the behavior of\0Athe program by using a distinct object for every value in the dictionary,\0Arather than a shared mutable instance. In some cases, programs may rely on\0Athe previous behavior.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `dict.fromkeys`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#dict.fromkeys)\0A", align 1
