@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1237 = private unnamed_addr constant [1099 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `logging.error` instead of `logging.exception` when\0Alogging an exception.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`logging.exception` logs the exception and the traceback, while\0A`logging.error` only logs the exception. The former is more appropriate\0Awhen logging an exception, as the traceback is often useful for debugging.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport logging\0A\0A\0Adef func():\0A    try:\0A        raise NotImplementedError\0A    except NotImplementedError:\0A        logging.error(\22Exception occurred\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport logging\0A\0A\0Adef func():\0A    try:\0A        raise NotImplementedError\0A    except NotImplementedError:\0A        logging.exception(\22Exception occurred\22)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as safe when run against `logging.error` calls,\0Abut unsafe when marked against other logger-like calls (e.g.,\0A`logger.error`), since the rule is prone to false positives when detecting\0Alogger-like calls outside of the `logging` module.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `logging.exception`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/logging.html#logging.exception)\0A", align 1
